From: Ryan Hughes <ryan@heyoodle.com>
Date: Sat, 10 Oct 2026 19:30:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] cpufreq: CPPC: don't register the artificial energy model on
 the NVIDIA N1x

The N1x firmware gives its Cortex-X925 and Cortex-A725 cores different
MADT efficiency classes, so cppc_cpufreq registers its artificial energy
model and EAS turns on. That model charges each higher class the energy
of four CPUs: in debugfs the A725 states cost 13 to about 30 and the X925
states 221 and up, so EAS keeps everything that fits on the A725 cores.
Interactive work is bursty and never raises their utilization far, so
it runs at or near the A725 floor of 338 MHz.

On an ASUS ProArt P14 that made the whole desktop lag. Hyprland stalled
for 20 to 50 ms at a time (libinput logged "event processing lagging
behind ... your system is too slow"), animations stuttered and typing
lagged. Measured as the round trip of Hyprland's IPC socket every 15 ms,
in power-profiles-daemon's balanced profile (CPPC autonomous selection,
EPP 64), with kernel.sched_energy_aware toggled between runs:

  EAS on:  p50 0.76-0.91 ms, p99 4-16 ms, worst 20-39 ms
  EAS off: p50 0.52-0.63 ms, p99 2.1-2.3 ms, worst 3.2-3.5 ms

Battery draw differed by 0.5 to 2.4 %, within the noise of the reading.
With EAS off the scheduler still prefers idle X925 cores by capacity,
and frequency comes from schedutil or the firmware's autonomous
selection as before.

Writing kernel.sched_energy_aware=0 from userspace is not a dependable
substitute: the write fails with EOPNOTSUPP whenever EAS is not possible
at that moment, and once it fails the default of 1 is kept. Skip the
artificial EM on these cores instead, so EAS never starts.
---
 drivers/cpufreq/cppc_cpufreq.c | 20 ++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 20 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/cpufreq/cppc_cpufreq.c b/drivers/cpufreq/cppc_cpufreq.c
--- a/drivers/cpufreq/cppc_cpufreq.c
+++ b/drivers/cpufreq/cppc_cpufreq.c
@@ -567,6 +567,21 @@
 			cpu_data->shared_cpu_map, 0);
 }
 
+/*
+ * The artificial EM charges each higher efficiency class the energy of four
+ * CPUs. On the NVIDIA N1x and GB10 (Cortex-X925 with Cortex-A725) that makes
+ * the X925 cores look over ten times as expensive as the A725 cores, so EAS
+ * packs interactive work onto the A725 cores at their lowest frequencies.
+ * The platform's firmware can also select performance autonomously, which
+ * the EM cannot model. EAS saves no measurable power there and stalls the
+ * desktop, so don't register an EM on these CPUs.
+ */
+static const struct midr_range cppc_no_artificial_em_cpus[] = {
+	MIDR_ALL_VERSIONS(MIDR_CORTEX_X925),
+	MIDR_ALL_VERSIONS(MIDR_CORTEX_A725),
+	{}
+};
+
 static void populate_efficiency_class(void)
 {
 	struct acpi_madt_generic_interrupt *gicc;
@@ -585,6 +600,11 @@
 		return;
 	}
 
+	if (is_midr_in_range_list(cppc_no_artificial_em_cpus)) {
+		pr_info("No artificial EM registered for these CPUs\n");
+		return;
+	}
+
 	/*
 	 * Squeeze efficiency class values on [0:#efficiency_class-1].
 	 * Values are per spec in [0:255].
